<ResourceDictionary xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
                    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
                    xmlns:ui="clr-namespace:DiamondDesktop">

  <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       GRADE AND SIZE, EVERYWHERE.

       Every way a grade or a sieve is picked or printed in this app, in one file. It is the Sales
       entry treatment, unchanged — the same templates, the same item containers, the same 34px
       field, the same search behaviour — lifted out of MainWindow so every other page can use the
       one definition instead of half of it.

       WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. These four resources lived in MainWindow.Resources, which is a scope
       nothing outside MainWindow can see. Sales entry had the full treatment; the other seventeen
       pickers were assembled a piece at a time — the templates re-attached from code-behind in two
       foreach loops, the height and the search behaviour repeated at every site in markup, and the
       accessible name simply missing on several. UpdateInvoiceDialog referenced these four by name
       while its own header comment records that MainWindow's resources are invisible to it.

       So the rule is now: a grade or size picker takes GradePicker or SizePicker and NOTHING ELSE.
       A grade or size CELL takes GradeCellText or SizeCellText. Nothing is set per-site, so nothing
       can be forgotten per-site.

       NOT A BEHAVIOUR CHANGE. Every value below is what Sales entry already used.
       ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <!-- ── what an item reads as ──────────────────────────────────────────── -->

  <!-- Shared so the closed combo and its dropdown can never disagree. See the Sales entry Size
       column for why DisplayMemberPath is not used with this design's combo template. -->
  <!-- ShortName, not Code. The catalogue stores a fifth of a carat as the number 0.2; the
       office has only ever called it 1/5, and so does the sheet the stock is counted on. -->
  <DataTemplate x:Key="SizeCodeTemplate">
    <TextBlock Text="{Binding ShortName}" ToolTip="{Binding Code}"/>
  </DataTemplate>

  <!-- The grade as the office writes it — "1BB", "#", "TOP co". Used by every grade combo in the
       app: Sales entry, Intake, the ledger, the dashboard filter and the price editor. The Stock
       filter builds its own strings and matches this deliberately, so a grade reads the same
       wherever it is picked. The code stays in the tables, which is where it is needed. -->
  <!-- No TextTrimming here. This template feeds the combo's CLOSED state as well as its list, and
       in the closed state it renders inside a presenter that reports almost no width while
       measuring — with trimming on, the whole name was ellipsised away and the cell came out
       blank. The column is sized to fit the longest grade name instead; the tooltip covers a
       longer one arriving later. -->
  <DataTemplate x:Key="GradeNameTemplate">
    <TextBlock Text="{Binding ShortName}" ToolTip="{Binding DisplayName}"/>
  </DataTemplate>

  <!-- The accessible name has to live on the CONTAINER. WPF's ComboBoxItemAutomationPeer names an
       item from the item itself, and with an ItemTemplate there is nothing to read, so it falls
       back to ToString() on the model — a database wire type. Every entry in the Grade list
       announced itself to a screen reader as "DiamondDesktop.Data.Grade", twenty-three times.
       Putting the name on the TextBlock inside the template does NOT fix it; the peer never
       looks there. Measured, not assumed. -->
  <Style x:Key="GradeItemContainer" TargetType="ComboBoxItem" BasedOn="{StaticResource FieldComboBoxItem}">
    <Setter Property="AutomationProperties.Name" Value="{Binding ShortName}"/>
  </Style>
  <Style x:Key="SizeItemContainer" TargetType="ComboBoxItem" BasedOn="{StaticResource FieldComboBoxItem}">
    <Setter Property="AutomationProperties.Name" Value="{Binding ShortName}"/>
  </Style>

  <!-- ── the pickers ────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->

  <!--
    A grade picker, whole. ItemsSource is the only thing a page still supplies, because that is
    data; everything below is what the control LOOKS and BEHAVES like, and no page has an opinion
    worth having about that.

    34px matches every other field on the operations forms — it is what all fifteen of these were
    setting by hand, one at a time.

    PickerSearch.On is set HERE rather than at each site. It was the single most-forgotten piece:
    a picker without it is a picker you have to scroll through a twenty-three-row catalogue to use,
    and there is no visible difference to notice it by.

    NOT for the Stock page's two filters. Those are filled with plain strings — "All grades", then
    the codes — and an ItemTemplate binding ShortName renders a string as nothing at all. They keep
    PickerSearch.On and the same implicit ComboBoxItem style, so they already read identically; the
    only thing they cannot take is a template for a property a string does not have.
  -->
  <Style x:Key="GradePicker" TargetType="ComboBox" BasedOn="{StaticResource AppComboBox}">
    <Setter Property="Height" Value="34"/>
    <Setter Property="ui:PickerSearch.On" Value="True"/>
    <Setter Property="ItemTemplate" Value="{StaticResource GradeNameTemplate}"/>
    <Setter Property="ItemContainerStyle" Value="{StaticResource GradeItemContainer}"/>
  </Style>

  <Style x:Key="SizePicker" TargetType="ComboBox" BasedOn="{StaticResource AppComboBox}">
    <Setter Property="Height" Value="34"/>
    <Setter Property="ui:PickerSearch.On" Value="True"/>
    <Setter Property="ItemTemplate" Value="{StaticResource SizeCodeTemplate}"/>
    <Setter Property="ItemContainerStyle" Value="{StaticResource SizeItemContainer}"/>
  </Style>

  <!-- ── the same two things, PRINTED in a grid ─────────────────────────── -->

  <!--
    A grade or sieve in a table cell. The mark the office writes, with what it stands for on the
    tooltip — so a column stays narrow and nothing is actually lost.

    The bindings are on the STYLE, not on the column, which is what makes this shareable: every
    row this is applied to must expose GradeShort/GradeName or SizeShort/SizeCode. That is already
    true of v_stock_position and v_sales_line, and the price grid's projection now matches them
    rather than printing the raw stored code.
  -->
  <Style x:Key="GradeCellText" TargetType="TextBlock">
    <Setter Property="ToolTip" Value="{Binding GradeName}"/>
  </Style>
  <Style x:Key="SizeCellText" TargetType="TextBlock">
    <Setter Property="ToolTip" Value="{Binding SizeCode}"/>
  </Style>

</ResourceDictionary>
